de-plugin/plugin.json+2-1Lines changed: 2 additions &amp; 1 deletionOriginal file line numberDiff line numberDiff line change@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@33  &quot;skills&quot;: [44    &quot;./skills/context-management/context-save&quot;,55    &quot;./skills/context-management/context-restore&quot;,6-    &quot;./skills/guardrails/claude-code-guardrails&quot;6+    &quot;./skills/guardrails/claude-code-guardrails&quot;,7+    &quot;./skills/engineering/commit&quot;78  ]89}Collapse file‎README.md‎Copy file name to clipboardExpand all lines: README.md+7Lines changed: 7 additions &amp; 0 deletionsDisplay the source diffDisplay the rich diffOriginal file line numberDiff line numberDiff line change@@ -21,6 +21,12 @@ Skills for saving and restoring session context — so you never lose your train2121- **[context-save](./skills/context-management/context-save/SKILL.md)** — Captures a compact snapshot of your session state (task, progress, decisions, next actions) and writes it to `.pi/`, `.claude/`, and `.codex/context.md` so any tool can pick up where you left off.2222- **[context-restore](./skills/context-management/context-restore/SKILL.md)** — Reads the saved context file, validates it against current git state, surfaces any discrepancies, and gets you back to work in seconds.232324+### Engineering25+26+Day-to-day code work skills.27+28+- **[commit](./skills/engineering/commit/SKILL.md)** — Create a focused git commit with a conventional message. Approval-gated, anti-force-push guardrails built in.29+2430### Guardrails25312632Always-on principles that keep the agent honest.@@ -42,6 +48,7 @@ The default agent behavior is to jump straight to code. These guardrails enforce4248## Philosophy43494450- **Tool-agnostic** — Skills write to `.pi/`, `.claude/`, and `.codex/` so you&#39;re never locked in to one agent51+- **Approval-gated** — Destructive operations (commits, force-push) always require explicit sign-off4552- **Codebase is truth** — Context files are memory aids, not authority. Always verify against actual state.4653- **Small and composable** — Each skill does one thing well. Mix and match with skills from other authors.4754- **Explicit invocation** — User-invoked skills only fire when you type the command. No surprise triggers.Collapse file‎skills/engineering/README.md‎Copy file name to clipboard+9Lines changed: 9 additions &amp; 0 deletionsDisplay the source diffDisplay the rich diffOriginal file line numberDiff line numberDiff line change@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@1+# Engineering2+3+Skills for day-to-day code work.4+5+## Model-invoked6+7+The agent can reach for these automatically when the task fits, or you can invoke them directly.8+9+- **[commit](./commit/SKILL.md)** — Create a focused git commit with a conventional message. Approval-gated, anti-force-push guardrails built in.Collapse file‎skills/engineering/commit/SKILL.md‎Copy file name to clipboard+49Lines changed: 49 additions &amp; 0 deletionsDisplay the source diffDisplay the rich diffOriginal file line numberDiff line numberDiff line change@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@1+---2+name: commit3+description: Create a git commit with conventional message format4+disable-model-invocation: false5+---6+7+# Commit Workflow8+9+Create a focused git commit with a conventional message.10+11+## Steps12+13+1. Run `git status` to see all changes14+2. Run `git diff --staged` to see staged changes15+3. Run `git diff` to see unstaged changes16+4. Analyze changes and draft commit message:17+   - Summarize what changed (not how)18+   - Use imperative mood (&quot;Add feature&quot; not &quot;Added feature&quot;)19+   - Keep first line under 50 characters20+   - Add body if needed for context21+5. Show proposed commit message and ask for approval22+6. If approved:23+   - Stage relevant files (`git add`)24+   - Create commit with the message25+   - Show result26+27+## Commit Message Format28+29+```30+&lt;type&gt;: &lt;short description&gt;31+32+&lt;optional body explaining why, not what&gt;33+```34+35+### Types36+37+- `feat`: New feature38+- `fix`: Bug fix39+- `refactor`: Code change that neither fixes nor adds40+- `docs`: Documentation only41+- `test`: Adding or fixing tests42+- `chore`: Maintenance, dependencies, config43+44+## Rules45+46+- Never commit without explicit user approval47+- Never use `--force` or destructive operations48+- Don&#39;t commit secrets, credentials, or .env files49+- Keep commits focused (one logical change)0 commit commentsComments0 (0)  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n    \n  \n\n  \n\n          \n  Footer\n\n  \n\n\n  \n    \n      \n        \n    \n\n\n      \n        &copy; 2026 GitHub,&nbsp;Inc.\n      \n    \n\n    \n      Footer navigation\n\n      \n\n\n          \n            Terms\n          \n\n          \n            Privacy\n          \n\n\n            \n              Security\n            \n\n            \n              Status\n            \n\n          \n            Community\n          \n\n          \n            Docs\n          \n\n          \n            Contact\n          \n\n          \n\n  \n    \n      Manage cookies\n    \n  \n\n\n  \n    \n      \n        Do not share my personal information\n      \n    \n  \n\n      \n    \n  \n\n\n\n\n    \n\n\n\n\n  \n    \n    \n\n    \n      \n    \n\n    \n    You can’t perform that action at this time.",
